## Shared or private, and the arithmetic most people get wrong

This is the decision everything else follows from, and it is not really about comfort. It is about how many of you there are.

A **shared desert safari** puts you in a Land Cruiser with five or six other guests and charges **per person**, from AED 99 for the evening package. A **private desert safari** gives you the vehicle, the driver and your own pace, and charges **per vehicle**, from AED 650.

Two people sharing pay AED 198. The same two on a private safari pay AED 650, which is a lot of money for quiet. But six people sharing pay AED 594, and the private Land Cruiser is AED 650 for the whole car. For AED 56 more, that group travels alone, leaves when it wants and nobody sits in the back row.

The crossover sits at about five people. Below it, share. Above it, work out both numbers before you book, because the per person habit hides the answer.

## Evening, morning or overnight

The evening safari is the one most people picture: pickup in the afternoon, dune bashing as the light drops, a sunset stop, then a Bedouin camp with halal BBQ dinner, a camel ride, sandboarding and the belly dance, tanoura and fire show. Seven to eight hours door to door, and the drive is a genuine part of that.

The [morning desert safari](/desert-safari-dubai/morning-desert-safari/) is four to five hours, runs in cooler sand, and puts you in a dune field with almost nobody else in it. There is no camp dinner and no show. What you get instead is the desert without an audience, and you are back with the rest of your day.

The overnight safari is the evening package plus a night at camp and sunrise over the dunes, sixteen hours at AED 250 per person.

**The seasonal answer overrides all of this.** From June to September, take the morning. An afternoon in the open desert in a Dubai summer is not a pleasant experience, and the red sand photographs identically at either end of the day.

## What is included, and the two things that surprise people

Every package we run includes hotel pickup and drop-off, dune bashing in a 4x4, a camel ride, sandboarding and the evening meal where the package has one. **Hotel pickup inside Dubai is free** on every booking. Transfers from Sharjah, Ajman, Abu Dhabi and Ras Al Khaimah are quoted with the booking.

Two things catch guests out, and neither is hidden, it just never gets said clearly on a listing page.

The first is the drive. Seven to eight hours sounds like seven to eight hours in the desert. It is closer to four, with roughly an hour of road at each end. If that matters to you, the morning safari has a better ratio.

The second is the camp. On a shared package the camp is shared, which is the point of it: a hundred or more people, a stage, and a queue at the buffet. The [VIP evening safari](/desert-safari-dubai/) at AED 150 per person moves you to a quieter section with a better spread. A [private safari](/desert-safari-dubai/private-desert-safari/) changes your vehicle, not the camp.

## Where it goes matters more than whose name is on the door

Nearly every desert safari sold in Dubai runs to the [Lahbab red dunes](/desert-safari-dubai/red-dune-desert-safari/). We run our base at Al Awir on the Dubai-Hatta road at the edge of that dune field, and so do most of the operators you are comparing us against.

That is worth knowing because it removes a whole category of marketing from your decision. Nobody has a secret desert. What genuinely differs between two packages at the same price is the state of the vehicle, whether the driver reads his passengers or drives the same way for everyone, and which camp you are dropped at.

If an operator is describing the dunes rather than the camp and the car, they are describing something they do not control.

## What guests tell us they regret

Booking the cheapest afternoon slot in August. It is the single most common one, and it is why we push the morning from June onwards even though the evening sells better.

Booking shared when six of them were travelling together, then working out the private number afterwards.

Not saying that someone in the group did not want aggressive dune bashing. Our drivers will run a gentler line if they know, and a shared car makes that harder because the rest of the vehicle wants the opposite. If anyone is pregnant, has a bad back or gets carsick, say so when you book and take a private vehicle or the [safari without dune bashing](/blogs/dubai-desert-safari-without-dune-bashing/). It is a two second conversation that decides whether four people enjoy the evening or three do.

